  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1117 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nThe strong upper ridge that brought well above normal temperature\nthe past few days will shift to the east on Friday. This will\nhave little to no impact on high temperatures over the Midsouth \nFriday and Saturday, as low level warm advection takes over where\nupper level ridging left off. Overnight lows will be milder,\nhowever, due to a marginal increase in low level mixing and\nincreased humidity in a modified tropical maritime airmass\n(especially Sunday morning). 12Z LREF mean PWAT depiction ranges \nfrom 1 to 1.2 inches around daybreak Sunday.\n\nA gradually weakening positive-tilted upper trough will enter the\nmid-MS River Valley Sunday morning, while the bulk of height\nfalls and surface cold front pass through the Midsouth during the\nafternoon and early evening on Sunday. Medium range models as\nwell as the 00Z RRFS-MPAS indicate scattered warm sector (pre-\nfrontal) showers over the Midsouth Sunday morning into the\nafternoon. A few thunderstorms are depicted later in the day,\nassociated with frontal forcing along and east of the MS River. \nBy such time, MUCAPE, as depicted by the RRFS-MPAS, ranges from \n250-500 J/kg, accompanied by modest midlevel lapse rates \n(5-6C/km) and marginal deep layer shear (30kt). In summary, \nadequate parameters for thunder, but likely not supportive of an \norganized severe storm threat.\n\nCooler and dry air will filter into the Midsouth Sunday night,\nbringing an end to our mid-November warm spell. Medium range\nguidance spread is rather wide with respect to the track of a\nclosed upper low during the midweek. At this time, NBM guidance\ntemps are depicted above freezing on the relatively warm eastern\nside of the low over the TN River Valley Wednesday morning. But\ngiven the wide envelope of ensemble solutions, the TN Valley cold\nrain scenario will likely change as we move closer to next week.",
